schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Community Readiness Model (nine-stage readiness assessment)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A nine-stage framework (from "no awareness" through "preplanning,"
    "preparation," "initiation," to "community ownership") for assessing
    how ready a community actually is to take on a new initiative, developed
    for public health work and applied here to launching a local ReFi node.
  source_traditions:
    - Tri-Ethnic Center for Prevention Research, Colorado State University (public health readiness research)
    - adrienne maree brown's "emergent strategy" (complex systems developing through experimentation and relationship, not master plans)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Used in is-community-ready.md to set an honest launch threshold ("stage
    5, preparation": leadership emerged, active planning, people mobilizing)
    below which a node is being pushed onto a community that hasn't decided
    it wants one.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - local-node-refi
    - emergent-strategy
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      "Interested" is distinguished from "ready" — the article insists on
      5-7 people who are *already showing up*, not people who might join.
    - >-
      Readiness is a stage on a spectrum, not a binary yes/no gate.
  tensions:
    - >-
      Wanting to talk a community out of premature launch vs. not gatekeeping
      genuine grassroots energy that's simply framed informally ("Web3 is
      interesting" vs. a felt shared problem).
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    A model built for public-health behavior change is applied here to
    financial-system adoption without validating that the same nine stages
    transfer cleanly; the article itself only claims it "travels well," not
    that it was validated for this context.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/is-community-ready.md
    transformation: synthesized
    authorship: ai-assisted
